In `MoeFCGemm::run_kernel_` (`cpp/tensorrt_llm/cutlass_extensions/include/cutlass_extensions/gemm/kernel/moe_cutlass_kernel.h`, current main):

```cpp
typename Mma::IteratorA iterator_A(
    LayoutA(ldm_A), ptr_A, {problem_size.m(), problem_size.k()}, thread_idx, tb_offset_A);
```

`LayoutA(ldm_A)` turns into a temporary `IteratorA::Params` that is destroyed at the end of the statement, but `PredicatedTileAccessIterator` keeps `Params const &params_`, so every later `++iterator_A` / `add_tile_offset` reads a dead object. Same for `iterator_B`, and for the scale iterator built with `initialize_scale(LayoutScaleZero(ldm_Scale), ...)` in the dq path.

With nvcc the stack bytes happen to survive and it works. With clang at -O2/-O3 the loads from the dead object are treated as undefined and most of the cp.async copies that load A and B read from garbage addresses. We hit it through FlashInfer's copy of this file: `CUDA_ERROR_ILLEGAL_ADDRESS` on the first bf16 `cutlass_fused_moe` call (DeepSeek-V2-Lite shapes, sm_121a), and compute-sanitizer reports invalid 16-byte global reads in `MoeFCGemm<MmaMultistage<GemmShape<32,128,64>, ...>>`. In clang's PTX for `moe_gemm_kernels_bf16_bf16.cu`, 2370 of the 2520 `cp.async` copies (in all 75 kernels that use `cp.async`) take their source address from a register that is never written, at -O2 and at -O3; at -O1, none do. With the change below, none do at -O3.

Giving the Params a name removes the illegal address for us (compute-sanitizer memcheck on the same call: 1356 errors before, every one it printed an invalid 16-byte global read; no memory errors after):

```cpp
typename Mma::IteratorA::Params params_A{LayoutA(ldm_A)};  // braces: with parens this declares a function
typename Mma::IteratorA iterator_A(
    params_A, ptr_A, {problem_size.m(), problem_size.k()}, thread_idx, tb_offset_A);
typename Mma::IteratorB::Params params_B{LayoutB(ldm_B)};
typename Mma::IteratorB iterator_B(
    params_B, ptr_B, {problem_size.k() * kInterleave, problem_size.n() / kInterleave}, thread_idx, tb_offset_B);
// dq path
typename Mma::IteratorScale::Params params_scale{LayoutScaleZero(ldm_Scale)};
```

With this change the call no longer faults, but correct output under clang also needed a second fix, in `moe_cute_util.cuh` (`util_convert_type` returns a view of a local array), reported in https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM/issues/19943.

FlashInfer carries a copy of this file with the same code; reported there too. CUTLASS's own `gemm/kernel/gemm_grouped.h` uses the same pattern.

### Which component has the problem?

CUTLASS C++

### Bug Report

**Describe the bug**

With CUDA NVCC 13.3.73, using a structured binding to unpack a tuple of CuTe TMA copy objects and then using the binding names in kernel template arguments causes `cudafe++` to generate an unexpectedly large host-side `.cudafe1.cpp` file.

The same source compiles successfully with NVCC 13.0.88. With NVCC 13.3.73, replacing the structured binding with an ordinary tuple variable plus `std::get<N>` also compiles successfully.

This reproduces with current CUTLASS `main` at `147295a3d4b75f3aeff247c25b8927cea9a7006a` (version header reports 4.8.0), so it does not appear to be fixed by a newer CuTe header revision. The evidence points to a CUDA 13.3 `cudafe++` regression, but I am reporting it here because the reproducer uses public CuTe TMA types.

**Steps/Code to reproduce bug**

```cuda
#include <tuple>
#include <cute/tensor.hpp>

using namespace cute;

template <class Tensor>
auto make_three_tmas(Tensor tensor) {
  using T = bfloat16_t;
  using KAtom = GMMA::Layout_K_SW128_Atom<T>;
  using MNAtom = GMMA::Layout_MN_SW128_Atom<T>;
  using QLayout = decltype(tile_to_shape(KAtom{}, make_shape(_128{}, _192{})));
  using KVLayout = decltype(tile_to_shape(KAtom{}, make_shape(_128{}, _192{}, _2{})));
  using YLayout = decltype(tile_to_shape(MNAtom{}, make_shape(_64{}, _128{})));

  auto q = make_tma_copy(SM90_TMA_LOAD{}, tensor, QLayout{});
  auto kv = make_tma_copy(SM90_TMA_LOAD{}, tensor, take<0, 2>(KVLayout{}));
  auto y = make_tma_copy(SM90_TMA_STORE{}, tensor, YLayout{});
  return std::make_tuple(q, kv, y);
}

template <class Q, class KV, class Y>
__global__ void probe(Q, KV, Y) {}

void reproduce(void* ptr, int rows, int cols, int heads, int ld) {
  using T = bfloat16_t;
  auto tensor = make_tensor(make_gmem_ptr(static_cast<T*>(ptr)),
                            make_shape(rows, cols, heads),
                            make_stride(ld, _1{}, cols));
#ifdef WORKAROUND
  auto tmas = make_three_tmas(tensor);
  auto q = std::get<0>(tmas);
  auto kv = std::get<1>(tmas);
  auto y = std::get<2>(tmas);
#else
  auto [q, kv, y] = make_three_tmas(tensor);
#endif
  auto kernel = probe<decltype(q), decltype(kv), decltype(y)>;
  kernel<<<1, 1>>>(q, kv, y);
}
```

Compile from a CUTLASS checkout:

```bash
nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 \
  -gencode arch=compute_90a,code=sm_90a \
  --expt-relaxed-constexpr \
  -DCUTE_SM90_EXTENDED_MMA_SHAPES_ENABLED \
  -Iinclude --keep --keep-dir keep \
  -c repro.cu -o repro.o
```

The workaround build adds `-DWORKAROUND`.

Measured results with the public reproducer:

| NVCC | Source form | Result | Generated `repro.cudafe1.cpp` |
| --- | --- | --- | ---: |
| 13.0.88 | structured binding | succeeds | 13,135,532 bytes |
| 13.3.73 | structured binding | stopped after exceeding a 64 MiB safety limit | >64 MiB |
| 13.3.73 | `std::get` workaround | succeeds | 13,446,890 bytes |

The 13.3 failing process was intentionally terminated once its generated host file exceeded 64 MiB to avoid unbounded disk usage. In the original larger translation unit, it produced a roughly 29 GB `.cudafe1.cpp` file before subsequent host compiler parse errors.

**Expected behavior**

NVCC 13.3 should compile the structured-binding form as NVCC 13.0 does, without expanding the bound CuTe types repeatedly into a huge generated host source file.

**Environment details**

- Environment location: bare metal
- OS: TencentOS Server 4, Linux x86_64
- Host compiler: GCC 12.3.1
- Failing compiler: CUDA NVCC 13.3.73
- Working comparison compiler: CUDA NVCC 13.0.88
- CUTLASS: current `main`, commit `147295a3d4b75f3aeff247c25b8927cea9a7006a`, version 4.8.0
- C++ standard: C++20
- Target: `sm_90a`

**Additional context**

The original failure was first observed with GCC 14.3, but it also reproduces with GCC 12.3.1. Keeping the tuple object named and extracting its elements with `std::get<N>` avoids the CUDA 13.3 front-end expansion while preserving the types and generated kernel behavior.

If this is best tracked directly by the CUDA compiler team, guidance or internal routing to the appropriate NVCC owners would be appreciated.

## Summary

AIBrix Gateway has several gaps between its request parsers, generated model routes, deployed reserved routes, routing-strategy behavior, and the APIs exposed by recent SGLang and vLLM releases.

This umbrella issue tracks five focused changes:

- [ ] #2990
- [ ] #2991
- [ ] #2992
- [ ] #2993
- [ ] #2994

Each child issue should remain independently implementable and mergeable.

## Motivation

The Gateway request flow requires a request to:

1. match a reserved HTTPRoute;
2. enter the Envoy external processing service;
3. extract the model from the request body;
4. resolve and validate the routing strategy;
5. select a model route or target Pod;
6. process the upstream response according to its transport type.

A missing route, an incompatible routing algorithm, or an incorrect response classification can break this flow even when the individual request parser already exists.

Recent SGLang and vLLM APIs also introduce new scoring and multimodal endpoint requirements that should be handled explicitly.

## Agreed scope

### 1. Reserved route consistency

Add the currently missing reserved routes for:

- `/v1/messages`
- `/v1/audio/transcriptions`
- `/v1/audio/translations`

Include regression tests for the deployed route configuration.

### 2. Request body logging

Keep request-body diagnostics available at high verbosity while making normal logging safe:

- normal logs contain request ID, endpoint, parse reason, and body size;
- raw JSON is available only at `klog.V(6)`;
- raw body logging is capped at 4 KiB;
- multipart contents are never logged;
- parsed prompt/message fields follow the same verbosity policy.

### 3. PD endpoint compatibility

Initially allow PD routing only for:

- `/v1/chat/completions`
- `/v1/completions`

Return `400 Bad Request` for every other endpoint when its effective routing strategy resolves to PD. Do not silently fall back to another routing strategy.

### 4. SGLang Score API

Add model-based routing support for `POST /v1/score`. Support pointwise and setwise request forwarding without reimplementing SGLang's model-specific scoring validation.

### 5. Audio Speech API

Add model-based routing support for `POST /v1/audio/speech`. Treat successful responses as streamed binary data rather than language-model JSON or SSE.

## Design constraints

- Follow the existing Gateway request-dispatch structure.
- Do not introduce an endpoint registry or `EndpointSpec` abstraction.
- Keep all five child issues independently mergeable.
- Preserve unknown engine-specific request and response fields.
- Reject incompatible PD requests before routing or accounting side effects.
- Preserve existing behavior for unrelated endpoints and routing strategies.

## Non-goals

This umbrella issue does not include:

- replacing existing `PathPrefix` matches with `Exact`;
- adding HTTP method restrictions to existing routes;
- adding PD support for Responses or Messages;
- vLLM scale-out or EPD support;
- SGLang runtime role switching;
- multipart streaming redesign;
- Gateway or Envoy dependency upgrades;
- exposing engine management endpoints such as `/sleep`, `/wake_up`, `/release_kv_cache_memory`, `/pd_role_switch`, `/render`, or `/derender`;
- introducing a centralized endpoint metadata subsystem.

## Completion criteria

This umbrella issue is complete when all five child issues are closed.

Each child issue must:

- include regression coverage;
- cover relevant error paths;
- preserve unrelated compatibility behavior;
- document the verification commands actually run;
- update English and Simplified Chinese documentation when public behavior changes.

### Description

Blockwise-scaled GEMM applies the wrong scale factors for every split-K slice beyond the first: the scale lookup uses the CTA-local K-tile index and is never offset by the slice's position in the global K range.

The multistage mainloop indexes the scale tensors by `k_iter_idx`, the loop counter local to this threadblock:

```cpp
// include/cutlass/gemm/threadblock/mma_multistage_blockwise.h:243-250
int ldA = int(scale_A.layout().stride(0));
int k_block_idx = k_iter_idx;
if (k_block_idx >= ldA) {
  k_block_idx = ldA - 1;                       // clamps OOB reads only
}
float scale_factor = scale_A.at({block_m_idx, k_block_idx}) *
                     scale_B.at({block_n_idx, k_block_idx});
```

and the kernel passes only the M/N tile offsets down to the MMA:

```cpp
// include/cutlass/gemm/kernel/gemm_universal_blockwise.h:268-273
mma(gemm_k_iterations, accumulators, iterator_A, iterator_B, accumulators,
    params.scale_A, params.scale_B, threadblock_tile_offset.m(),
    threadblock_tile_offset.n());
```

There is no `threadblock_tile_offset.k()` contribution anywhere, so slice `s` (which multiplies A/B blocks from global K range `[s * tiles_per_slice, (s+1) * tiles_per_slice)`) scales all of its partial products with the scale factors of K-blocks `[0, ...)`. The clamp at line 245 turns out-of-range lookups into silent reuse of the last scale block instead of a diagnostic.

The wrapper accepts this configuration when `kSplitKSerial` is true (`device/gemm_blockwise.h:367-369` rejects `split_k_slices > 1` only for the non-serial path), so the mis-scaled result is returned as success.

### Suggested fix

Offset the scale index by the slice's global K-tile base (`threadblock_tile_offset.k() * gemm_k_size / ThreadblockShape::kK`, or equivalent) before indexing `scale_A`/`scale_B`, keeping the existing clamp as a bounds guard.

### Which component has the problem?

CuTe DSL

### Bug Report

## Description

During AOT compilation an explicit `preferred_smem_carveout` survives compilation and is stored in the exported object's metadata, but the generated C loader does not apply it. This reproducer exports the same kernel with preferences of **25%** and **75%**. Both exports set attributes **8** and **14** (via `_cudaFuncSetAttribute(...)`), but omit attribute **9** (which is `PREFERRED_SHARED_MEMORY_CARVEOUT`).

This feature was introduced in 4.6.0, [link here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/discussions/3385#discussion-10422374)

## Environment

- `nvidia-cutlass-dsl` and its components: **4.8.0**
- `cuda-bindings`: **13.3.1**
- Python **3.10.12**, Linux **x86-64**
- GPU compilation target: **`sm_90a`**
- Host object target: **`x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, `x86-64` CPU**
- GCC **11.4.0**, GNU binutils **2.38**

## Reproduce

Point: compile AOT some kernel with some explicit values for `preferred_smem_carveout`, then compare disassembled object files and see no `_cudaFuncSetAttribute(...)` with values for attribute **9** (PREFERRED_SHARED_MEMORY_CARVEOUT) but identical loaders.

